> {[}(S, φ){]}/\(\mathcal{G}_{\text{sub}}\) --- they rest on the local
> spatial/gauge construction of §2.3 and on cubic-group representation
> theory, each entry under the further conditions of the theorems cited
> for it; whether an entry requires finiteness of the substratum is not
> established where those theorems use the measure conditions of Lemma 1c
> or Proposition 4b or the continuum conditions of the \(d = 3\) selection
> (§3.2), and \(1/\alpha_0\) is computed from the lattice momentum
> integral of §6.1 over the whole Brillouin zone, whose counterpart for a
> finite substratum and whose normalization of the vertex coupling are not
> established. These include, conditionally on H-link with its single-copy
> clause and H-cust (§4.5--§4.6): SU(3) × SU(2) × U(1) with multiplicities
> (3, 2, 1), whose reduction from the stabilizer U(3) × U(2) × U(1) of
> Theorem 5 carries the amplitude-scale-gauge and background-independence
> premises of §4.6, a specified charged matter action and the further
> physical input of Proposition 14a (§4.9), three generations (spin-1/2
> sectors under H-spin', Theorems 9--10; chirality conditional on H-χ',
> §4.8; light-spectrum selection, Theorem 10) with identical gauge quantum
> numbers, one Higgs doublet (1, 2, +1/2), taken as input and accommodated
> in the E projection of the singlet taste (Theorem 11), unique
> hypercharges from anomaly cancellation, given the observed family
> pattern in the five representations per family, family-universal charges
> and chirality under H-χ' (Theorem 15; apart from the exceptional branch
> \(Y_Q=0\)), and the universal induced coupling \(1/\alpha_0 = 23.25\)
> (§6.1). These are theorems on the branch stated in §1 (Tier 2), with the
> conditions marked at each entry. The horizon calibration
> \(\hbar = c^3\epsilon^2/(4G\sigma)\) and the Bekenstein-Hawking
> coefficient it matches hold for the same class as consistency relations
> conditional on H-count, which includes finiteness of the total system
> and a conserved energy shell, and on H-slope on identified transitions,
> H-frame, the H-Hawking horizon and frame conditions, local boost
> structure and the symmetric detailed-balance rate ratio, with selection
> of the physical slope an additional condition ({[}GR §2.3, §3.2, §4--§5,
> §8.5{]}). They do not depend on which specific φ describes our universe.
